## Solution

**Claim.** The unique solution is
$$f(p,q,r) = \begin{cases} \dfrac{3\,pqr}{p+q+r} & \text{if } p+q+r > 0, \\[6pt] 0 & \text{if } (p,q,r)=(0,0,0). \end{cases}$$

---

### 1. Setup and reduction to slices

Let $n = p+q+r$. Each of the six arguments appearing in the recurrence differs from $(p,q,r)$ by adding $+1$ to one coordinate and $-1$ to another, so **$n$ is invariant** under every transition. Hence the recurrence decouples across the slices $\{(p,q,r) : p,q,r \ge 0,\; p+q+r = n\}$.

On the slice with sum $n$, the interior is $D_n = \{(p,q,r) : p,q,r \ge 1,\; p+q+r = n\}$ (nonempty iff $n \ge 3$), and the boundary $\partial D_n$ consists of triples with at least one coordinate equal to $0$, where $f = 0$.

Define the averaging operator
$$ (Af)(p,q,r) = \tfrac{1}{6}\bigl[f(p{+}1,q{-}1,r)+f(p{-}1,q{+}1,r)+f(p{-}1,q,r{+}1)+f(p{+}1,q,r{-}1)+f(p,q{+}1,r{-}1)+f(p,q{-}1,r{+}1)\bigr]. $$
The recurrence reads $f = 1 + Af$ on $D_n$, i.e. $-\Delta f = 1$ where $\Delta := A - I$ is the discrete Laplacian, with $f|_{\partial D_n} = 0$.

---

### 2. Uniqueness (maximum principle)

Suppose $h$ satisfies $h = Ah$ on $D_n$ and $h = 0$ on $\partial D_n$. The slice is a **finite** connected graph (the triangular lattice inside a triangle). Let $M = \max_{D_n \cup \partial D_n} h$. If $M > 0$, the maximum is attained at some interior point $x$ (since $h=0$ on the boundary). There $h(x) = Ah(x)$, i.e. $h(x)$ equals the average of its six neighbors, so every neighbor also attains the value $M$. Iterating, $M$ propagates to the boundary, contradicting $h=0$ there. Thus $M \le 0$; similarly $\min h \ge 0$, giving $h \equiv 0$. So the solution on each slice is unique. (Existence follows, e.g., from the expected hitting time of the boundary by the simple random walk, which is a finite absorbing chain.)

---

### 3. A Laplacian computation

For the monomial $m(p,q,r) = pqr$ (with $n = p+q+r$), expand the six neighbor values:

$$\begin{aligned}
(p{+}1)(q{-}1)r &= pqr - pr + qr - r,\\
(p{-}1)(q{+}1)r &= pqr + pr - qr - r,\\
(p{-}1)q(r{+}1) &= pqr + pq - qr - q,\\
(p{+}1)q(r{-}1) &= pqr - pq + qr - q,\\
p(q{+}1)(r{-}1) &= pqr - pq + pr - p,\\
p(q{-}1)(r{+}1) &= pqr + pq - pr - p.
\end{aligned}$$

All the mixed terms ($\pm pr, \pm qr, \pm pq$) cancel in pairs, leaving
$$\sum_{\text{6 nbrs}} pqr = 6\,pqr - 2(p+q+r) = 6\,pqr - 2n.$$
Therefore
$$-\Delta(pqr) = pqr - \tfrac{1}{6}(6\,pqr - 2n) = \frac{n}{3}. \tag{$\star$}$$

---

### 4. Verification of the candidate

On the slice with sum $n \ge 3$, set $f := \dfrac{3}{n}\,pqr$. Since $n$ is constant on the slice, by linearity of $\Delta$ and $(\star)$:
$$-\Delta f = \frac{3}{n}\bigl(-\Delta(pqr)\bigr) = \frac{3}{n}\cdot\frac{n}{3} = 1.$$
Equivalently, $f = 1 + Af$ on $D_n$. On the boundary $\partial D_n$ (some coordinate $=0$), $pqr = 0$, so $f = 0$. At the origin $(0,0,0)$ we set $f = 0$ directly, consistent with the $pqr = 0$ condition.

Concretely, summing the six neighbor values of $f$:
$$\sum_{\text{6 nbrs}} f = \frac{3}{n}\sum_{\text{6 nbrs}} pqr = \frac{3}{n}(6\,pqr - 2n) = \frac{18\,pqr}{n} - 6 = 6f - 6,$$
so $\tfrac{1}{6}\sum f_{\text{nbrs}} = f - 1$, i.e. $1 + \tfrac{1}{6}\sum f_{\text{nbrs}} = f$. ✓

---

### 5. Conclusion

The candidate satisfies the recurrence on every interior point and the boundary condition $f = 0$ whenever $pqr = 0$. By the uniqueness established in Step 2 (applied slice by slice), it is the **only** such function. Hence the unique solution is

$$\boxed{\,f(p,q,r) = \dfrac{3\,pqr}{\,p+q+r\,}\ \text{for } p+q+r>0,\qquad f(0,0,0)=0.\,}$$

